{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfppDmbVQ631M34nKBQ6V","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Happy to pause there — tests are the right place to stop, for a reason I'll come back to.\n\nThe framing I'd push back on first: \"done\" and \"needs a human judgment call\" aren't two ends of one scale. They're independent. Work can be verifiably complete and still need a judgment call (it works; should we ship it?), and it can be badly incomplete while needing zero judgment (just more grinding). Treating them as one axis is what produces both failure modes at once — agents that stop to ask permission for mechanical work, and agents that silently make product decisions.\n\nThe test I actually use is: **is the remaining uncertainty resolvable by evidence I can go get, or does it require a preference I can't derive?** Empirical questions — does this handle the input, what does the existing code do, does the build pass — I should answer myself, and asking is just offloading. Preference questions have no fact of the matter in the repo; investigation doesn't converge. Those are the human's.\n\nThen a second axis cuts across it: cost of being wrong. A preference question that's cheap to reverse shouldn't block — pick a default, say so out loud, keep moving. Only *unresolvable and expensive to reverse* earns a hard stop. The quadrant people under-use is unresolvable-but-cheap, where the right move is a stated assumption rather than a question.\n\nOur slugify case is a decent live test, and I over-asked on one of three. Unicode genuinely warranted stopping: ASCII-strip vs. Unicode-aware vs. transliteration are three different implementations with different test suites, so guessing wrong means rewriting the tests, not editing a regex. Underscores was borderline — cheap to flip, I'd already said which reading I'd take, I could have just taken it. Non-string input I shouldn't have raised at all: `trim` lets `.trim()` throw, so the codebase already answered it. I had the evidence and asked anyway.\n\nOn \"done\" — the useful move is splitting it into three things that usually get conflated:\n\n- **Complete**: all the specified pieces exist. Mechanically checkable.\n- **Correct**: it behaves as specified. Checkable by execution — and this is where *failing for the right reason* matters. Our six tests failed with `TypeError: slugify is not a function`, not with a wrong assertion. A red test that's red for the wrong reason will go green later and tell you nothing.\n- **Right**: the spec itself was the right spec. I cannot check this. It's irreducibly yours.\n\nWhen an agent says \"done\" it almost always means *complete*. When a human hears it they usually infer *right*. That gap is most of the trouble.\n\nWhich is the real argument for stopping here rather than ritual TDD: green tests prove the code matches *my reading* of the spec. If the reading is wrong, passing tests are a more confident wrong answer, and when the same agent writes both the tests and the implementation, the tests inherit the implementation's misconceptions. Reviewing tests before implementation is the cheapest point to catch a bad reading — the artifact you'd throw away is small.\n\nOne risk worth naming before we formalize anything: a doneness criterion is an incentive, not just a measurement. \"Done = tests pass\" produces tests that pass. \"Done = no open questions\" produces fewer questions asked. So the criterion should point at artifacts a third party can inspect, not at self-reports.\n\nIf you want something falsifiable, I'd suggest the report always separate **verified** (with the evidence), **assumed** (the default I picked and how to flip it), and **blocked** (the specific question). Empty assumed-list on a non-trivial task means I'm hiding decisions. More than one or two blocked items means I'm offloading.\n\nWhere do you want to push on this — the resolvable/preference split, or the reversibility axis? I suspect the second is where it gets genuinely hard, because reversibility is often misjudged in the cheap direction."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10394,"cache_read_input_tokens":12800,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10394},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1e76c9a-f4e3-45df-acaf-fbadb722aed6","uuid":"fc1a02d4-d2ec-4889-b6b7-07629ad56c77","timestamp":"2026-10-08T13:03:35.909Z","request_id":"req_011CfppDmMqmMKobUtA2tsje"}
